Z||000011||theogre||01-27-2001||12:09 AM||||They recomend electric because the loose powder is flamable. <P>The electric elements should have been enough. You only need to hit around 300F max. It's more important that the heat be even in the box. <P>The propane could be handy for preheating but I think I'd shut it off before shoving a part in there. Propane can be a damp heat. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||DXR_DAD||01-27-2001||12:53 AM||||Ogre, thanks I had that pre-heat thing in mind, but it's a big box and I want it to be multi-use (pig roaster)?<P>Steve, Don't talk about the PG&E thing out here????? we may all need LP when it's over.<BR>It may have a few bugs to work out but I WILL BE COATING SOON!<P><BR>THANKS GUYS<BR>DXR_DAD<p>[This message has been edited by DXR_DAD (edited 01-27-2001).]||||reg||13
Z||000013||Dan86gt3.8||01-27-2001||04:24 AM||||I worked at a powder coating company for a couple summers and the oven that they had was about 40 feet long by about 10 feet high.  it was big enough to do car frames, and i think it even did a frame from an rv once.  I mean it was a defenitely walk in kind of deal.  As far as the powder and stff I don't really know anything about it.  I just sanblasted, preusre washed, and preped the metal (anything thats to be powder coated needs to be clean metal no oil or rust).  I am not trying to knock your do it yourself thing.  its actaully pretty cool.  It just made me rember how huge that oven they had was.  I wish I had my cradle done when I did my engine swap.  I ended up just painting it...<P>------------------<BR>86 gt fiero<BR>3.8/4speed auto<BR>ace conversion kit<BR>1/4- 15.3@90mph||||reg||1
